  <info>## WHAT IS IT?

This is a predator-prey model that investigates extinction risks under habitat loss. Predators hunt prey, while prey try to find food, avoid predators, and avoid destroyed habitat.

The world consists of food patches. During the simulation, circular areas of habitat loss appear and turn into brown no-food patches. Prey cannot reduce their hunger on these patches, so they try to avoid them. If prey or predators become too hungry, they die.

The model is used to study how habitat loss, starvation, predation, reproduction, herding, escape behaviour, and crowding affect extinction risk.
## HOW IT WORKS

At setup, the model creates green food patches, predators, and prey. Each animal receives a hunger value.

During each tick, predators search for nearby prey and move toward them. If no prey are nearby, they move randomly. Prey flee from nearby predators, avoid no-food patches, and may herd with other prey.

Prey hunger increases on no-food or overgrazed patches and decreases on good food patches. If prey hunger becomes too high, they die. Predators become hungrier each tick. When a predator catches prey, it eats one prey, resets its hunger, and may reproduce. If predator hunger becomes too high, it dies.

Prey can reproduce when they are well-fed, but reproduction is limited by local prey density. If too many prey are close together, crowding stress increases their hunger.

Habitat loss occurs after random time intervals. A circular area of patches becomes destroyed and can no longer provide food. The simulation stops when either predators or prey go extinct.

## HOW TO USE IT

Press setup to create the world, predators, and prey. Press go to run the simulation. The go button should be set to forever.

The slider n_preds controls the initial number of predators. The slider n_preys controls the initial number of prey.

max-pred-hungry and max-prey-hungry control how much hunger predators and prey can tolerate before dying.

predator-reproduction-chance and prey-reproduction-chance control how likely each species is to reproduce.

min-habitat-loss-radius and max-habitat-loss-radius control the size of habitat-loss events. min-habitat-loss-time and max-habitat-loss-time control how often habitat loss occurs.

predator-vision controls how far predators can see prey. prey-vision controls how far prey can detect predators. herd-radius and herd-chance control prey herding behaviour.

max-local-prey-density controls how many nearby prey are tolerated before crowding stress occurs. crowding-hunger-cost controls how much extra hunger crowding causes.

Useful monitors are: prey population, predator population, habitat lost percentage, mean prey hunger, mean predator hunger, trapped prey, extinction tick, and extinct population.

## THINGS TO NOTICE

Watch how brown no-food patches spread through the world and reduce usable habitat.

Notice whether prey extinction happens gradually or suddenly. Rising mean prey hunger can be an early warning signal.

Observe whether predators die out after prey decline because they lose their food source.

Pay attention to herding. Herding may help prey escape predators, but it can also create dense groups and increase crowding stress.

Notice how habitat loss forces prey into smaller areas, increasing starvation and extinction risk.

## THINGS TO TRY

Try increasing habitat-loss radius or making habitat loss happen more frequently. This should increase extinction risk.

Try increasing prey reproduction chance and observe whether prey recover or overpopulate.

Try increasing predator vision or predator reproduction chance and observe whether predators overhunt prey.

Try increasing prey vision and see whether prey survive longer.

Try changing herd chance to test whether herding helps or harms prey.

Try increasing crowding hunger cost to reduce unrealistic prey population explosions.

Run the same settings several times, because randomness can produce different extinction outcomes.

## EXTENDING THE MODEL

The model could be extended by adding habitat recovery, so destroyed patches can become usable again after some time.

Food could be represented with a numerical food variable instead of patch color.

Prey and predators could have different life stages, such as young and adults.

Habitat loss could spread from existing destroyed areas instead of appearing randomly.

The model could also include protected areas, corridors, or barriers.

## NETLOGO FEATURES

(interesting or unusual features of NetLogo that the model uses, particularly in the Code tab; or where workarounds were needed for missing features)

## RELATED MODELS

(models in the NetLogo Models Library and elsewhere which are of related interest)

## CREDITS AND REFERENCES

(a reference to the model's URL on the web if it has one, as well as any other necessary credits, citations, and links)</info>
